  exports and explain that iCloud export is a manual snapshot.

Bank credentials are not included in backups. Automatic iCloud synchronization
and automatic assistant access to files were not added.

[04] RECURRING PAYMENT DATES AND REMINDERS
Status: Implemented; new date regression tests supplied but not run here.

- Added an optional recurrenceAnchor field to preserve the intended day.
- Fixed monthly date drift after short months: January 31 can advance to
  February 28 and then return to March 31.
- Preserved February 29 as the intended yearly date so it can return in a
  later leap year.
- Preserved the anchor when editing an unchanged schedule and reset it when
  the due date or cycle changes.
- Used the anchor when advancing paid bills and calculating upcoming reserves.
- Sorted notification candidates by due date and limited scheduling to the
  60 nearest future reminders.
- Corrected account help text: manual balance entry accepts zero or positive
  values; recorded spending or bank snapshots may produce negative balances.
  Credit-card debt is not modeled.

Recurring payments and local reminders existed before these changes.
Notification delivery on an iPhone remains unverified.

[05] BANK BALANCE DUPLICATE PREVENTION
Status: Implemented; new Swift regression test supplied but not run here.

- Added an optional bankAccountKey combining provider environment and bank
  account ID.
- Applying a known bank account's balance now targets its existing matched
  ledger account instead of allowing another copy of the same cash account.
- On first application, the user can create an account or match an eligible
  unlinked manual account.
- Added ledger validation that rejects duplicate bank-account keys.
- Preserved the provider snapshot retrieval time when applying a balance.
- Kept the new data fields optional for compatibility with older ledgers.

[06] PLAID LOGIN REPAIR AND BACKEND REQUEST HANDLING
Status: Implemented; backend checks passed with a mocked Plaid provider.

- Added "Load Existing Connections" and a per-connection "Repair Login"
  action to the bank-connection interface.
- Added client response models for saved bank connections.
- Added GET /v1/connections to return saved connection IDs and display names,
  without exposing saved access tokens.
- Extended POST /v1/link-token to accept an optional itemID.
- Repairing a known connection creates a Link token in update mode using its
  existing saved access token and omits new-link products.
- Unknown connection IDs return 404 before a provider request is made.
- Successful update-mode repair does not exchange a new public token.
- New-link requests retain their products and depository account filters.
- Added a new-link confirmation explaining existing-login repair and Trial
  connection-slot usage.
- Cleared the displayed saved connections after configuration changes and
  successful disconnection.
- Added optional backend institution display names, trimmed to 100 characters.
  The current app does not supply this label, so generic connection names can
  still appear.
- Rejected malformed JSON, null/array/non-object bodies, and oversized request
  bodies with HTTP 400 instead of a generic service failure.
- Changed ITEM_LOGIN_REQUIRED guidance to direct users to Load Existing
  Connections, then Repair Login.

The original backend already had authentication, encrypted token storage,
refresh throttling, persistence, and retryable failed disconnections. This
continuation added coverage for those behaviors; it did not originate them.
No live bank was connected and no backend was deployed in this environment.

